{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XLwoJUBM6gztwP5GG47MoK","type":"tool_result","content":"### 2.2 Which structural quantities determine the optimum?\n\n`reports/learning.md` §1 already shows structure alone predicts the optimum\nexactly on 71.2% of held-out instances against the proved bound's 65.3%, and\nthat MOSP-graph edges and mean degree carry the signal, not matrix shape. The\nopen question is *what function* of the graph it is.\n\n- **Method:** extend the feature set with graph invariants that pathwidth is\n  known to relate to — treewidth upper bounds (min-fill, min-degree), bandwidth\n  heuristics (Cuthill–McKee), spectral radius and Fiedler value, balanced\n  separator size from a cheap partitioner, clique cover number, and the random\n  intersection graph parameters `(n, m, p)` themselves. Then symbolic regression\n  and monotone GAMs on the optimum and, separately, on the **residual over the\n  best proved bound**. A GNN on the MOSP graph as the \"is anything missing\"\n  check.\n- **Baseline:** the current 36-feature LightGBM, grouped by file.\n- **Deliverable:** a ranked list of invariants, and if one simple formula fits\n  the residual, a written conjecture of the form \"for these graphs,\n  `pw ≥ f(...)`\" handed to the bound work. That is the route by which the\n  expansion bound was found, formalised.\n- **Kill:** if no invariant beyond the current set moves grouped MAE by more\n  than 0.02, the current features already capture what is capturable at this\n  scale, and the section closes with that statement.\n\n----\n## 4. What would count as understanding\n\nNot model accuracy. The outputs that would change what this project knows:\n\n- a graph invariant, or a formula in `(n, m, p)`, that tracks the optimum better\n  than the proved bounds, stated as a conjecture with the instances it was tested\n  on — a target for a proof, or for the Lean development;\n- a description of the bound-defeating family in words, with small drawn\n  examples;\n- a hardness map with an order parameter, locating the benchmark classes on it;\n- a readable closing rule that recovers most of what imitation learned;\n- the size at which each of the above stops holding.\n\nA model that predicts well and explains nothing is a tool, and\n`learning_plan.md` already decides what tools are worth. This plan is for the\nother thing.\n----\n113:## 4. What is closed\n114-\n115-Do not rebuild these. Each is measured, reported, and has a report explaining\n116-the mechanism rather than just the outcome:\n117-\n118-- **learned upper bounds** as a route to faster solving (`reports/learned_search.md` §2)\n119-- **learned or learned-adjacent lower bounds** as a route to faster solving (`reports/expansion_bound.md` §6)\n120-- **learned branching inside the search** (`reports/expansion_bound.md` §7)\n121-\n122-The artefacts stay, behind off-by-default flags, with their numbers attached.\n123-`learned+cs-dfs` remains the best upper-bound heuristic here and is the right\n124-thing to run if the corpus ever re-opens with instances a ratchet must attack.\n125-\n126-## 5. The rule that does not change\n127-\n128-**A prediction is never a bound.** `_lower_bound` is a correctness dependency: a\n129-bound one point above the true optimum makes the search start above it and\n130-return a wrong answer whose witness verifies. Nothing learned may enter that\n131-path. Orderings a model proposes are checked by `max_open_stacks` like any\n132-other, which is what makes §3.1 safe and §3.4 honest.\n133-\n134-## 6. One premise that was wrong on its own terms\n135-\n136-`reports/learning.md` opens by saying the corpus is unusual because its labels\n137-are proofs rather than a solver's best effort — supervised learning with no\n138-label noise.\n139-\n140-**One of those labels was wrong.** `Random-100-100-2-2_0` was recorded as\n141-optimal at 21; the optimum is 20. It was certified by a refutation that a bug\n142-made false, and nothing downstream could detect it.\n143-","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12577c41-7101-4638-b007-8765a211c262","uuid":"9576aa84-efbd-41b6-b258-884bb9994499","timestamp":"2026-09-25T19:30:12.281Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### 2.2 Which structural quantities determine the optimum?\n\n`reports/learning.md` §1 already shows structure alone predicts the optimum\nexactly on 71.2% of held-out instances against the proved bound's 65.3%, and\nthat MOSP-graph edges and mean degree carry the signal, not matrix shape. The\nopen question is *what function* of the graph it is.\n\n- **Method:** extend the feature set with graph invariants that pathwidth is\n  known to relate to — treewidth upper bounds (min-fill, min-degree), bandwidth\n  heuristics (Cuthill–McKee), spectral radius and Fiedler value, balanced\n  separator size from a cheap partitioner, clique cover number, and the random\n  intersection graph parameters `(n, m, p)` themselves. Then symbolic regression\n  and monotone GAMs on the optimum and, separately, on the **residual over the\n  best proved bound**. A GNN on the MOSP graph as the \"is anything missing\"\n  check.\n- **Baseline:** the current 36-feature LightGBM, grouped by file.\n- **Deliverable:** a ranked list of invariants, and if one simple formula fits\n  the residual, a written conjecture of the form \"for these graphs,\n  `pw ≥ f(...)`\" handed to the bound work. That is the route by which the\n  expansion bound was found, formalised.\n- **Kill:** if no invariant beyond the current set moves grouped MAE by more\n  than 0.02, the current features already capture what is capturable at this\n  scale, and the section closes with that statement.\n\n----\n## 4. What would count as understanding\n\nNot model accuracy. The outputs that would change what this project knows:\n\n- a graph invariant, or a formula in `(n, m, p)`, that tracks the optimum better\n  than the proved bounds, stated as a conjecture with the instances it w